body:
We don't put this detailed docstrings in headers usually. There's different opinions for how much comments we should have throughout the codebase, and arguably we could use more, but here it introduces an inconsistency with hundreds of other APIs in the codebase which doesn't seem warranted.

These descriptions are also the kind that IMO don't provide much value, as what these methods do is pretty self-evident from the prototype. I'd keep comments to only point out what is worth knowing which isn't obvious from the prototype.

In this case what I would personally find useful reading this class is to have a general explanation of the purpose of these methods (as a paragraph before declaring the methods, not on each method as it would be redundant), notably pointing out that `encode_input_event` and `decode_input_event` are the main APIs that can handle any event time, the other ones are just type-specific implementations used in the main ones.

```suggestion
// Helper functions to encode or decode input events as byte arrays,
// for serialization/deserialization purposes.
//
// The main `encode_input_event`/`decode_input_event` can take any event type.
// The other encoding methods are type-specific implementations used in `encode_input_event`.

bool encode_input_event(const Ref<InputEvent> &p_event, PackedByteArray &r_data);

// Note: If the event type is not recognized, the event will remain invalid.
void decode_input_event(const PackedByteArray &p_data, Ref<InputEvent> &r_event);

void encode_input_event_key(const Ref<InputEventKey> &p_event, PackedByteArray &r_data);
```

